INT. CLOSED PLANETARIUM - NIGHT

The dome glows with a frozen field of stars. Rows of reclining seats, one of them occupied by a dead man in a tuxedo. DET. ISADORA FENN (40s, rain-slick trench) crouches by the body while patrolman OKAFOR (20s, notebook) points his flashlight at the carpet, the museum director MR. HALVERSEN (60s, silk scarf, trembling) hovers by the doors, and a tall STRANGER (30s, charcoal suit, no umbrella) lingers at the back, watching all of them.

                    FENN
          (to everyone)
          Nobody touches the console. Nobody leaves the dome.

                    HALVERSEN
          He was only here to give a lecture. On Orion.

                    FENN
          (to Okafor)
          Photograph his hands. He's gripping something.

Okafor kneels and photographs. The dead man's fingers hold a small brass key. Fenn turns to the back row.

                    FENN (CONT'D)
          You. Who are you?

                    STRANGER
          Anselm Rook. I was his guest.

                    FENN
          Mr. Rook, you're standing very still for a man whose host just died.

                    ROOK
          I'm counting. The stars on the dome are wrong. Somebody reprogrammed the sky.

Halverson steps back. Fenn looks up and sees it too: one constellation reversed.

                    FENN
          (to herself)
          That's not Orion. That's a signature.
